# gitlab-runner-privileged (infrastructure tier)

The **trusted** runner: root, Docker-in-Docker, unrestricted egress. It exists so
this repository's own CI can deploy the cluster it runs on — Ansible over SSH,
kubectl, secret injection, container builds.

## The registration scope IS the security boundary

> **Register this runner as a PROJECT runner locked to the infrastructure
> repository. Never as an instance runner. Never as a group runner over a group
> that contains untrusted projects.**

Tags are cooperative routing, not access control. Any project on the instance can
write `tags: [{{ ci_runner_tag }}]` in its `.gitlab-ci.yml`. If this runner is
registered instance-wide, that one line gets an arbitrary authenticated user root
+ DinD execution on your cluster, on a runner that also has SSH reach to your
hosts. That is a privilege-escalation path into the whole estate.

Nothing in this repository can enforce that — the scope lives in GitLab, not in
a manifest — and an unchecked one has already gone wrong: in the system this
template generalizes the docs stated the rule while the live registration was
`instance_type`, and another project's pipeline ran on it.

**Verify after registering**, and again after any runner re-registration:

```
GET /api/v4/runners/<id>   ->  "runner_type": "project_type"
```

If you want tenant repositories to build images, register a *second* privileged
runner scoped to the tenant group, with its own namespace, quota and egress
policy, and a distinct tag (`tenant-build`). Do not widen this one.

## Isolation from the shared runner

- **Separate namespace.** The shared runner's namespace-scoped RBAC cannot read
  this runner's token Secret. Namespace separation — not the tag — is what makes
  that true.
- **Separate ServiceAccount.** Job pods run as `gitlab-runner-privileged-jobs`
  (`serviceaccount.yaml`) — no RoleBinding, `automountServiceAccountToken:
  false` — not the chart-managed manager SA, whose Role can read the runner
  token Secret and exec into concurrent job pods. A job that genuinely needs the
  kube-API brings an explicit kubeconfig.
- **`enforce: privileged` PSS**, because the DinD job pods genuinely require it.
  This is exactly why it must not be the namespace untrusted jobs land in.
- **`<label-domain>/runner-class` is the TIER, not the tag.** Job pods here are
  labelled `runner-class=infrastructure`, the shared runner's are labelled
  `runner-class=shared`, and three things key off those two fixed values: the
  pod anti-affinity in `release.yaml`, the unrestricted-egress NetworkPolicy in
  `networkpolicy.yaml`, and kured's `--blocking-pod-selector`. Renaming the CI
  tag (`ci_runner_tag`) deliberately does **not** move them — that is why the
  tag beside them is generated while these stay literal.

`protected: false` is deliberate and, unlike the shared runner's, is a real
residual risk: it lets this privileged runner execute MR pipelines from
unprotected feature branches. It is accepted because every job in this repo's
pipeline is tagged for this runner and MR CI would otherwise never run. **The
project-lock above is what keeps that risk bounded to committers on this one
repository.**

## Sizing

The governor is CPU, not memory. Per job, the real cost is roughly: build
container ~0.9 cpu (the Ansible control process), DinD sidecar ~0.5 cpu, helper
~0.1 — about 1.5 cpu, while memory is a few hundred MiB.

Two rules follow, and both have a failure mode with no obvious cause:

- **`cpu_request` must reflect real usage.** Understate it and the scheduler
  packs more jobs onto a node than its cores can run; task-heavy jobs then take
  several times as long, because the exec-per-task connection model crawls under
  CPU starvation.
- **`concurrent` must stay at or below the schedulable pool.** Above it the
  overflow parks as *Pending pods*, and pod-Pending time burns the JOB's timeout
  clock — jobs get killed without ever starting. At or below pool capacity the
  overflow waits in GitLab's queue, where that clock does not run.

Size `concurrent` to `(CI-eligible cores - co-tenant requests) / 1.5`, and keep
`requests.cpu` in the ResourceQuota in lockstep.

The shipped values are that formula applied to the roster this cluster was
generated for: {{ compute_node_count | default(2) | int }} compute {{ 'host' if compute_node_count | default(2) | int == 1 else 'hosts' }} x one 4-core k3s agent each, minus 2 cores for the
platform pods co-tenanting them (job pods are hard-excluded from the NAS agent,
so it is not in the pool). Grow the agents or their cores and both numbers must
be raised by hand — nothing recomputes them after generation.

## Scheduling

Job pods are **hard-excluded** from the storage node (`DoesNotExist` on its
label, not `NotIn` — which would require the key to exist on every node). A
memory-tight storage host forced to swap other guests' pages because a CI burst
landed there is a real and expensive failure. Soft pod anti-affinity spreads jobs
across the remaining agents, because simultaneous same-node DinD container starts
race the cgroup setup and die at prepare.

## Configure

| Value | Where |
|---|---|
| Runner token | secrets item **GitLab Runner Privileged** → `runner-token` |
| Tag | `{{ ci_runner_tag }}` — generated from the `ci_runner_tag` answer on BOTH sides (this runner's `tags:` and every job's `tags:` in `.gitlab-ci.yml`), so they cannot drift. With `runUntagged: false`. |
| Job image | `runners.config` TOML, digest-pinned |
| Concurrency | `concurrent` + `resourcequota.yaml` (together) |

## Disable

Remove the entry from `kubernetes/apps/kustomization.yaml`. Then move this repo's
own CI jobs to the shared runner tag — anything needing SSH, DinD or root will
stop working, which is the point of the tier.
